{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-924255f1ad30b2a1", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tFINDING THE NOISE: ZERO-SHOT AI MUSIC DETECTION\n2\tDarius Afchar\n3\t\n4\tRomain Hennequin\n5\tDeezer Research\n6\tresearch@deezer.com\n7\t\n8\tarXiv:2607.25530v1 [cs.SD] 28 Jul 2026\n9\t\n10\tABSTRACT\n11\tWe present a novel method for AI-generated music detection in scenarios where the models that generated the\n12\tinput samples are unknown to the detector (e.g., from a\n13\tnewly released service). Since 2023, there has been a multiplication of user-friendly AI-music generation services\n14\t(e.g., Suno, Udio), along with regular updates and new features. There is thus a need to address synthetic content\n15\tdetection in an unsupervised way to adapt to this rapidly\n16\tchanging context. This angle has not been much studied\n17\tin music yet. We propose to study two tasks. First, discriminating between real and synthetic music. This may\n18\tbe approached in a one-class manner, namely, using some\n19\tbaseline real music and trying to determine what falls outside. Second, zero-shot multi-class identification, which is\n20\tmore similar to an unsupervised clustering task on a mix of\n21\treal and various AI-music generations, where the goal is to\n22\tcreate coherent, high-purity clusters. We propose a combination of a previously proposed artifact-extraction method,\n23\ton top of which we apply non-negative matrix factorization and simple classification and clustering methods. We\n24\tachieve excellent performance on both tasks, showing that\n25\tthe proposed methods may be used to monitor large-scale\n26\tcatalogs that may receive AI-generated samples from various newly released generative models.\n27\t1. INTRODUCTION\n28\tSince the end of 2023, there has been a massification of AIgenerated music with the launch of services such as Suno\n29\tand Udio. These services follow the paradigm of popular tech services like OpenAI’s ChatGPT in that they propose to fully generate music in a matter of seconds from a\n30\ttext prompt, and are available online for free. Since then,\n31\tstreaming services have seen a steady increase in the volume of synthetic content delivered 1 . For instance, Deezer\n32\trevealed in June 2025 that 18% of the music they received\n33\tdaily was AI-generated [1]. This number is now nearing\n34\t50%, less than a year later [2]. The latter also reported that\n35\tmost of the listening of AI content was fraudulent. Many\n36\tmore AI music services have also emerged since then (e.g.,\n37\tProducers, ElevenLabs, Mureka).\n38\tIn this context, we need to explore AI music detection\n39\tmore. The goal of this paper is not to discuss the philosophical and musical implications of whether AI music\n40\tshould or should not be treated similarly to real music.\n41\t1 We use the term synthetic as a short-hand for whether an AI service\n42\t\n43\tproduced a music sample.\n44\t\n45\tIndeed, there are many ethical and legal challenges surrounding AI music services: e. g., how copyright and royalties should work with AI content, how to deal with hybrids, whether using copyrighted music falls under \"fair\n46\tuse\" [3–6]. Beyond these considerations, it seems critical to at least label AI content properly to inform listeners. This aligns with the transparency regulations around\n47\tAI [7, 8], as well as broader discussions on the proliferation of \"AI slop\", across many fields beyond music (e.g.,\n48\timages, news, comments, fake videos). We believe that\n49\tresearch on detectors may help push forward the conversation about the scale of the where, what, and how AI music\n50\tis published, which in turn, may help enlighten the aforementioned debates around generative content, the protection of artists, and the value of human creation [9–12].\n51\tThis topic remains relatively novel, and there is little\n52\tprior work on AI-music detection specifically (cf. Section\n53\t2). To our knowledge, prior work has focused on supervised detection. This leaves several research gaps open.\n54\tFor instance, how to deal with unknown music generation services? This question is also relevant for detectors trained on data from specific services that have been\n55\tupdated since training. For instance, the SONICS dataset\n56\t[13], used in MIREX 2025 [14], might already be considered deprecated, as it contains Suno v3.5 music samples,\n57\twhereas the latter has recently released v5.5. It thus seems\n58\tnecessary to explore more zero-shot techniques.\n59\tIn this paper, we present a novel method to achieve such\n60\tan aim. We define two tasks that we deem relevant to study\n61\tin the context of AI-music detection. First, discriminating\n62\tbetween real and synthetic music. This may be approached\n63\tin a one-class manner: having some real music and trying\n64\tto establish what falls outside. Second, blind multi-class\n65\tidentification, which is more similar to an unsupervised\n66\tclustering task on a mix of real and AI-music samples generated by various different models.\n67\tIn Section 2, we present the related work. In Section\n68\t3, we formulate the two tasks and propose to use a mix\n69\tof generative artifact extraction and non-negative matrix\n70\tfactorization. In Section 4, we conduct our experiments on\n71\tthree datasets of real and synthetic music. Our results are\n72\tpromising, achieving high accuracy on most datasets and\n73\ttasks, while requiring almost no supervision.\n74\t2. RELATED WORK\n75\tCurrently, there are relatively few work on the topic of AImusic detection [13, 15–21]. The topic is quite novel: the\n76\tfirst papers were published in 2024, following the release\n77\t\n78\t\fof Suno at the end of 2023 which dramatically increased\n79\tthe volume of AI-generated music uploaded to the internet [1,2,22–24]. As mentioned, all the previous cited work\n80\tregard supervised detection. To our knowledge, no paper\n81\thas yet explored unsupervised settings. Nevertheless, zeroshot learning and non-negative matrix factorization have\n82\tbeen successfully employed in many other music and signal processing tasks (e. g., [25–28]).\n83\tThe motivations behind the development of AI detectors may be put into perspective through discussion on the\n84\t\"AI Slop invasion\". There exists a Wikipedia page that\n85\tis quite exhaustive on that topic and listing many recent\n86\tcases around generative AI in music [29]: e. g., the Velvet\n87\tSundown and Breaking Rust bands that topped the charts.\n88\tStreaming service Deezer have also evidenced that a majority of streams on AI music found in their catalog turned\n89\tout to be fraudulent [6].\n90\tHowever, might all AI-music be slop? Several authors\n91\thave dissected the notions of creativity and authorship in\n92\tAI music with a more philosophical angle [4, 12, 30, 31],\n93\tas well as the sociotechnical context around the provider\n94\tof such services: e. g., their tendency for extractivist data\n95\tcollection, reliance on low-paid annotation labor, and risk\n96\tof job displacement for musicians [10, 11, 32–35].\n97\tBeyond music, the impact of generative AI on society, politics, fake news, work and culture is actively discussed [5, 9, 36–42]. Without opening too much the Pandora’s box on the potential risks, harms, and opportunities around generative AI, we can at least note that there is\n98\tglobal consensus on the need for greater transparency into\n99\tsynthetic generations. This principle been transcribed into\n100\tEuropean law in the Digital Service Act, and later the AI\n101\tAct, as well as in several other countries [7, 8].\n102\tOur contribution on AI music detection is motivated by\n103\tthis transparency goal. Our focus on unsupervised and\n104\tzero-shot setting stems from the current lack of research\n105\tin the existing literature.\n106\t3. METHOD\n107\tWe provide some background on AI-music artifacts, formalize the tasks we propose to study, and present our\n108\tmethod to achieve such an aim.\n109\t3.1 Background\n110\tTo understand the reasonings behind our method, we must\n111\tbriefly introduce the AI-music artifact extraction process\n112\tproposed in [17]. Simply put, the deconvolution layers of\n113\tconvolutional neural networks were shown by the authors\n114\tto leave small periodical energy peaks across all frequencies (i. e., \"checkerboard artifacts\" [43]). Their detection\n115\tmethod thus relies on extracting such peaks profile (e. g.,\n116\tsee Figure 1), and then learning a linear regression on top\n117\tof it on a dataset of real and synthetic samples.\n118\tTo obtain such profiles, a given music sample x is\n119\tprocessed in short-time Fourier transforms, and its power\n120\tspectrogram is computed. The latter is temporally averaged to even out the melodic information. On this mean\n121\t\n122\tFigure 1: Real and synthetic fakeprints. We plot for\n123\teach class a 100 random music fakeprints in transparent to\n124\tsee the distribution, one more sample in dotted black, and\n125\tthe average fakeprint in red. We zoom in on the frequency\n126\tband 3kHz-9kHz for readability.\n127\tpower curve, they subtract the local minima on sliding windows to extract the residual local peaks. The result for each\n128\tmusic sample is a curve representing the time-averaged local variations in power per frequency. A frequency cut is\n129\tapplied to only extract a band of interest, and finally, it\n130\tis normalized by its max to result in a representation in\n131\t[0, 1]d . We refer to this processing as fakeprint(x).\n132\tThe authors showed that this process was sufficient to extract the deconvolution artifacts in music tracks (if any are\n133\tpresent) and obtain near-perfect detection scores, while remaining fast to compute and directly interpretable.\n134\tIn this work, we propose to employ the fakeprint()\n135\tas a base representation for our zero-shot tasks. In Figure 1, we show the average fakeprints of Suno versus real\n136\tmusic. As it may be seen, synthetic fakeprints exhibit\n137\tthe expected regular peaks, and in all of their samples.\n138\tMeanwhile, real samples exhibit peaks—which are always\n139\tcreated by the normalization step—but their localization\n140\tseems uniformly scattered across frequencies between different samples. This results in an average fakeprint that is\n141\tquite flat, denoting an absence of structure in real music’s\n142\tfakeprints. This observed property is the key in our method\n143\tto distinguishing synthetic content (consistent peak pattern) versus real content (random peaks placement).\n144\t3.2 Tasks and assumptions\n145\tIn zero-shot learning tasks, the models do not use labels\n146\tin their training [44–46]. Instead, some other a priori information must be used on the data to separate them in a\n147\tway that makes sense (e. g., structural information, inductive biases, backbone pretrained on different data). Zeroshot may also mean learning on a subset of the labels, and\n148\ttrying to predict new unseen classes. In this spirit, oneclass classification describes a setting where samples from\n149\tonly one class are provided to the model [47–49]. The goal\n150\tis to learn the proper frontiers enclosing all given samples\n151\tand to assign a binary label to all the remaining samples as\n152\tbeing inside or outside of this support. In both cases, the\n153\tmodel is blind to a part of or all of the input labels.\n154\tThe a priori knowledge we propose to use in this paper\n155\tis that fakeprints may be a good representation of samples\n156\t\n157\t\ffor the downstream task of blind synthetic content detection. This is informed by the previously reported good performance in supervised settings. We also test this assumption in Section 4.3. We propose two variants of zero-shot\n158\ttasks that we find relevant to study in our context of AImusic detection with regular new services and versions:\n159\tTask A. We may first aim to distinguish real versus synthetic music in a binary way. We regroup all AI music under the target label \"synthetic\". One-class classification is\n160\tvery suited to this setting. Our goal is to learn the manifold\n161\tof real music among a collection of samples that includes\n162\treal music and several AI services music. We assume the\n163\tclass cardinality not to be too imbalanced. We also assume\n164\tto be able to estimate a good upper bound to the number of\n165\tall different classes that may be in a given dataset.\n166\tTask B. To have more granularity, we may then aim to\n167\tseparate all classes altogether, namely all AI services, but\n168\tthis may also regard different versions of that service that\n169\tmay exhibit different artifact patterns. There, the real class\n170\tis treated as one class among others. Zero-shot learning is\n171\tvery suited to this task, relying on a priori information and\n172\tstructure analysis to cluster the music samples. Note that\n173\tsome labeled samples and metadata may later be used to\n174\tinterpret each cluster (if properly separated).\n175\tAs we next explain, both tasks may be tackled with two\n176\tvariants of the same method.\n177\t3.3 Method for A (real versus synthetic)\n178\tAs hinted in the background section 3.1, the key principle we rely on is that synthetic samples will follow some\n179\tstructured peak profiles (e. g., roughly one per AI service)\n180\twhereas real music sample will exhibit random peak localizations, akin to random noise. Note that the \"structures\"\n181\twe seek arise from statistical analyses, but not from the\n182\tshape of the pattern themselves.\n183\tDictionary learning. With this in mind, our idea is to employ a dictionary learning method that will extract patterns\n184\tfrom the fakeprints. We denote as X ∈ [0, 1]n×d a preprocessed dataset of n fakeprints of length d containing real\n185\tand synthetic examples. Given that fakeprints are positive,\n186\twe propose to employ a non-negative matrix factorization\n187\t(NMF) [50, 51]. With the NMF, the goal is to approximate\n188\tX with a factorization HW . The matrix H ∈ Rn×f\n189\tis of+\n190\tten referred to as containing activation coefficients, while\n191\tW ∈ Rf+×d contains the atoms (or components), namely\n192\tthe dictionary of patterns that are learned from the data.\n193\tThe factorization parameter f is chosen as the upper bound\n194\tfor the total number of classes we expect to find in the data.\n195\tIn practice, we often have a reasonable estimate for f .\n196\tEven if f is chosen too high, we add ℓ1 and ℓ2 regularizations (i. e., an elastic net) in the NMF optimization:\n197\tmin ∥X − HW ∥22 + λH ∥H∥1,2 + λW ∥W ∥1,2\n198\t\n199\tH,W\n200\t\n201\twith ∥.∥1,2 = ∥.∥1 + ∥.∥22 . This enables to have a wellbehaved dictionary of atoms. We only want an atom to\n202\t\n203\tbe learned if a sufficient collection of fakeprints follow a\n204\tgiven pattern. Conversely, we do not want each unique\n205\tfakeprint of real music to be learned. Said differently, real\n206\tmusic is treated as random noise in the fakeprint representation. NMF has been used in audio with this denoising\n207\taim (e. g., [26]).\n208\tAfter the denoising effect of the NMF, does this mean\n209\tthat synthetic samples will all have positive activations in\n210\tH and a corresponding component in W , whereas real\n211\tsamples have null activations? Almost. After some preliminary experiments, it turns out that some atoms are learned\n212\tfor real music: flat profiles without localized peaks. This\n213\tmakes sense as denoising results in approximating the\n214\tmean envelope of real fakeprints. Therefore, we need one\n215\textra step to identify the fakeprints of real music.\n216\tDelocalized reconstruction error. Given the learned activations H and atoms W , our idea is that W will have\n217\tlearned some average peaks profiles: structured atoms with\n218\tlocalized peak for synthetic samples, and flat envelopes to\n219\treconstruct the noise of real music (similar to Figure 1).\n220\tWe propose to destroy the learned peak structures in W\n221\tby convolving the atoms with a Gaussian kernel G. Since\n222\tfakeprints represent mean power spectra, from a Fourier\n223\tperspective, this Gaussian blur may be interpreted as a\n224\tlow-pass filter in cepstra. Then, we compare the recone = HW and X\n225\te ′ = H(W ∗ G), and define\n226\tstructions X\n227\tei − X\n228\te ′ ∥2 . We\n229\tfor all sample of index i, the error ri = ∥X\n230\ti\n231\texpect ri to be low for real music and high for AI-music.\n232\tOur criterion r is also very reminiscent of edge detection\n233\tin computer vision. We can interpret it as finding whether\n234\te i have \"edges\", i. e., peaks.\n235\tthe reconstructions (X)\n236\tThreshold choice. So far, we have worked in a purely unsupervised manner. We now need to fix a threshold to distinguish whether ri correspond to a real or synthetic sample i. We may use some examples of real samples to calibrate this. This is where this method turns into a one-class\n237\tclassification. Following previous literature [48], given a\n238\tsmall training collection of real examples, we take a quantile of a training set of errors (ri ), for instance, 95%. This\n239\tallows to have a direct control of the false-positive rate in\n240\tthe detection of real music (e. g., 5%).\n241\t3.4 Variant for B (multi-class clustering)\n242\tIn the second setting we study, we may also try to have\n243\tmore granularity in the patterns we have learned. Coming\n244\tback to our proposed method in section 3.3, we have used\n245\ta NMF to distinguish atoms with localized peaks (synthetic class) versus atoms without localized information\n246\t(real class). But a NMF can also be directly interpreted\n247\tas learning a clustering. Therefore, we can already stop at\n248\tthis step to have a zero-shot clustering of the real and all\n249\tthe various possible AI-music classes.\n250\tSince the space learned in the NMF is latent and in high\n251\tdimension, we propose to add a UMAP to better visualize\n252\tthe learned representation [52]. This technique is usually\n253\thandy for separating clusters well, and when there is no\n254\t\n255\t\fneed to have a meaningful metric on the cluster size and\n256\ttheir relative distance. This method was also created to be\n257\teasy to parametrize, with only one main parameter, which\n258\tis suited for zero-shot learning. Since we do not know in\n259\tadvance the number of clusters, we propose to employ the\n260\tHDBSCAN clustering [53], which is also quite straightforward to use with minimal required information on the\n261\tdata. For task B, we have arguably proposed a much more\n262\tstraightforward combination of known techniques. We will\n263\tsee in the next section that this works well as is for AImusic identification.\n264\tDifferently from the previous section, the variant we\n265\tproposed for task B is purely zero-shot. We still have\n266\tsome main parameters to configure, which can often be\n267\testimated from the dataset size and the minimum expected\n268\tcluster sizes. We could use a few classes to tune these\n269\thyper-parameters and study the detection for the other unknown classes. Afterwards, if the clustering works well,\n270\ta few samples may also be employed to label the found\n271\tclusters, identify the real music cluster, assign to each a\n272\tknown music service, or conversely identify a new cluster\n273\twith an unknown artifact pattern (i. e., few-shot learning).\n274\tWe leave these options as future work and use reasonable\n275\tdefault parameters in this paper.\n276\t\n277\t4. EXPERIMENTS\n278\tWe present our datasets, some preliminary supervised results to check if the data is separable using the fakeprints,\n279\tand the experiments for our two tasks and method.\n280\t4.1 Datasets\n281\tAs exposed in Section 2, there are relatively few work\n282\tpublished on AI music detection, which also means few\n283\tpublished datasets (cf. the survey [54]). For our tasks, we\n284\tfirst consider the dataset from [16], which provided autoencoded samples of the Free Music Archive (FMA) [55]\n285\tthrough 4 models: \"Encodec, DAC, Musica, GrifMel\". In\n286\tthe original dataset, the autoencoded samples are available\n287\tin several compression settings. We only pick the highest\n288\tquality for each class. We use the \"small\" split of FMA\n289\twith 8000 samples per class. We refer to it as FMA-AE.\n290\tThe previous dataset does not contain any sample from\n291\tpopular AI services. We had first considered using the\n292\tSONICS dataset [13]—which was proposed in last’s year\n293\tMIREX challenge. However, it only contains two classes\n294\t(Suno and Udio). Even more critical, all audios in SONICS\n295\thave been resampled to 16kHz, which results in a unsuited\n296\tcutoff in the frequency bands of interest for fakeprints. Alternatively, we found a recent dataset in a publication still\n297\tin preprint: Echoes [21]. This dataset contains 10 classes\n298\tfrom online services: \"DiffRhythm, Suno, Brev, ACEStep,\n299\tProducer, Udio, SongGen, AudioLDM, Mubert, and StableAudio\". Despite its relatively small size (3577 tracks\n300\tin total), we found it interesting for our purpose to test our\n301\tmethod on a variety of different services. Since this dataset\n302\tdoes not provide real audio tracks, we resort to including\n303\t\n304\tsamples from the FMA, as was previously done in the literature [17]. All music tracks are resampled to 44.1kHz.\n305\tFinally, for completeness, we create a dataset including the two most popular services Suno and Udio, as well\n306\tas four more online services that had not been collected\n307\tin Echoes yet quite popular: Lyria 3, ElevenLabs, Mureka\n308\tand Riffusion. We fetch a few thousand tracks for each\n309\tof these services, as well as some metadata (e. g., subversions). We resample them all to 44.1kHz. We dub this\n310\tdataset PopularAISet 2 .\n311\t4.2 Settings\n312\tWe use the similar parameters to configure the fakeprints as\n313\tin the original paper (e. g., a n_fft of 214 for the STFT),\n314\tand a frequency band of [3kHz, 15kHz]. This results in\n315\tfakeprint vectors of length 4458.\n316\tIn Task A, the Gaussian kernel is picked to be \"quite\n317\tlarge\" relative to the single peaks we are trying to smooth,\n318\tnamely a radius 10 in our experiments. The number of\n319\tNMF components are set to 20 since we know that this was\n320\ta proper upper bound of the number of classes in our considered datasets. In Task B, the n_neighbors parameter\n321\tof the UMAP is arbitrarily fixed at 20. And the minimum\n322\tcluster size of the HDBSCAN is fixed at n/5f , an arbitrary\n323\tfraction of the average expected cluster size (n/f ).\n324\tWe use the same set of settings for all three datasets.\n325\tThe rest of the chosen parameters may be found on our\n326\tcode repository 2 .\n327\t4.3 Preliminary separability test\n328\tBefore moving to our two unsupervised tasks, we wanted\n329\tto verify this paper base assumption that the fakeprint representation was suitable and could linearly separate AI music tracks from real music tracks with the datasets we study.\n330\tCompared to [17], we test 18 different AI models in total, which includes many that the latter method has not\n331\tbeen evaluated on. This preliminary section thus serve as a\n332\tquick reproducibility check of past work, as well as a quick\n333\toverview on the extension of detection scores to models\n334\tuntested in the literature yet.\n335\tFollowing [17], we train logistic regressors on\n336\tfakeprints computed on the tracks of our datasets. Note\n337\tthat we operate in a supervised binary setting, not a multiclass one. We take the real class of each dataset and train\n338\ta regressor for each available synthetic class. The selected\n339\tdata is split in a 80%-20% fashion, and we compute the\n340\tequal error rate (EER) on the predictions.\n341\tThe results are provided in Table 1. First, we insist\n342\ton the fact that the Echoes dataset is very small. Therefore, after the train-test split, there only remain between\n343\t30 and 60 samples in each test sets. The displayed scores\n344\tfor Echoes should be taken with a grain of salt and are\n345\tto be read as a quick sanity check. Second, overall, the\n346\tresults in the supervised detection setup look very good,\n347\tand comparable to previous work. This is reassuring that\n348\tthe fakeprint does what we want to achieve. Finally, some\n349\t2 Will be made public after acceptance.\n350\t\n351\t\fDataset / class\n352\tFMA-AE\n353\tEncodec\n354\tMusika\n355\tDAC\n356\tGrifMel\n357\tPopularAISet\n358\tSuno (Pop.AISet)\n359\tUdio (Pop.AISet)\n360\tLyria 3\n361\tRiffusion\n362\tElevenLabs\n363\tMureka\n364\t\n365\tEER (↓)\n366\t0.1%\n367\t0.0%\n368\t0.0%\n369\t1.0%\n370\t1.3%\n371\t0.2%\n372\t0.3%\n373\t0.2%\n374\t2.0%\n375\t8.7%\n376\t\n377\tDataset / class\n378\tEchoes\n379\tSuno (Echoes)\n380\tUdio (Echoes)\n381\tBrev\n382\tStableAudio\n383\tACE-Step\n384\tAudioLDM\n385\tDiffRhythm\n386\tSongGen\n387\tProducer\n388\tMubert\n389\t\n390\tEER (↓)\n391\t0%\n392\t0%\n393\t0%\n394\t0%\n395\t0%\n396\t0%\n397\t0%\n398\t0%\n399\t0%\n400\t30%\n401\t\n402\tTable 1: Preliminary supervised binary detection\n403\tscores. The scores for Echoes are written with a minimal\n404\tprecision due to a lack of samples in the test sets.\n405\tspecific classes have significantly worse linear separation\n406\tscores that the others: Mubert in Echoes, and Mureka in\n407\tthe PopularAISet. We can anticipate those not to be well\n408\tdetected in the next unsupervised settings.\n409\t4.4 Results\n410\tWe present our results on the two tasks, for our method\n411\tand its variant. As reminder, Task A is binary and oneclass, while Task B is multi-class and zero-shot. Note that\n412\twe do not have any baseline model to compare our work to\n413\tsince this unsupervised angle is novel.\n414\tTask A: real versus synthetic. The only hyperparameter in our method A is the threshold on ri , which can be\n415\tinterpreted as a target false positive rate (FPR) to bound\n416\tthe observed distribution of the real class. In these experiments, we display the accuracy of detection for the synthetic classes, while targeting an FPR of 10% and 1%. To\n417\tachieve this, we compute these quantiles on half of the real\n418\texamples and use the rest as test. As a reminder, we did\n419\tnot use any of the synthetic data. We only discriminate the\n420\ttypical statistical patterns of real music from those that fall\n421\toutside a defined boundary and may not be real music.\n422\tThe results are presented in Table 2. As a first observation, without any information on the synthetic class, it is\n423\thard to anticipate whether a target FPR of 10% or 1% is\n424\t\"going too far\" and catching synthetic samples in the real\n425\tclass support: it worked in both cases on FMA-AE, but degraded the performance for an FPR at 1% for the other two\n426\tdatasets. The aggregated scores for the synthetic classes\n427\tdo not mean much as is, and the performances are better explained by looking at the breakdown for each model:\n428\tOverall, the results are very good for all AI services, but\n429\tfor the two models Mubert and Mureka that we had already\n430\tflagged in the linear separability test. Unsurpringly, the detection has worsened compared to Table 1: when the NMF\n431\tdenoised the fakeprints, the already poorly separated synthetic fakeprints were assigned to the flat atom 3 and thus\n432\tassigned the real label. Conversely, many results turned\n433\tout to be as good as in the supervised setting. For instance,\n434\tfor Suno in the PopularAISet, an EER of 1.3% seems to\n435\t3 ...which we could confirm by inspecting the learned activations H.\n436\t\n437\tDataset and class\n438\tFMA-AE\n439\tReal\n440\tSynthetic\n441\t,→ Encodec\n442\t,→ Musika\n443\t,→ DAC\n444\t,→ GrifMel\n445\tEchoes\n446\tReal\n447\tSynthetic\n448\t,→ Suno (Echoes)\n449\t,→ Udio (Echoes)\n450\t,→ Brev\n451\t,→ StableAudio\n452\t,→ ACE-Step\n453\t,→ AudioLDM\n454\t,→ DiffRhythm\n455\t,→ SongGen\n456\t,→ Producer\n457\t,→ Mubert\n458\tPopularAISet\n459\tReal\n460\tSynthetic\n461\t,→ Suno (PopularAISet)\n462\t,→ Udio (PopularAISet)\n463\t,→ Lyria 3\n464\t,→ Riffusion\n465\t,→ ElevenLabs\n466\t,→ Mureka\n467\t\n468\tAcc @10%\n469\t\n470\tAcc @1% (↑)\n471\t\n472\t90.0%\n473\t99.4%\n474\t99.9%\n475\t100%\n476\t99.9%\n477\t97.8%\n478\t\n479\t99.4%\n480\t98.1%\n481\t99.8%\n482\t100%\n483\t99.2%\n484\t93.5%\n485\t\n486\t90%\n487\t93%\n488\t100%\n489\t87%\n490\t100%\n491\t98%\n492\t100%\n493\t100%\n494\t100%\n495\t100%\n496\t100%\n497\t5%\n498\t\n499\t100 %\n500\t53 %\n501\t67%\n502\t3%\n503\t58%\n504\t4%\n505\t95%\n506\t83%\n507\t90%\n508\t46%\n509\t39%\n510\t0%\n511\t\n512\t90.4%\n513\t91.6%\n514\t99.9%\n515\t99.4%\n516\t98.8%\n517\t99.9%\n518\t93.3%\n519\t48.5%\n520\t\n521\t98.5%\n522\t80.4%\n523\t99.8%\n524\t96.4%\n525\t75.6%\n526\t99.6%\n527\t77.5%\n528\t14.9%\n529\t\n530\tTable 2: One-class detection accuracy scores on the\n531\tthree datasets at the chosen FPR 10% and 1%. For each\n532\tdataset, we highlight the aggregate scores as well as the\n533\tbreakdown per each AI generator model.\n534\tmatch the 98.5% for real versus 99.8% for Suno.\n535\tAll in all, these experiments are quite satisfying given\n536\tthe relative simplicity of our proposed criterion and\n537\tmethod. A 10% FPR threshold may be considered large\n538\tfor a real-world application (e. g., analyzing a streaming\n539\tmusic service catalog). However, the main motivation of\n540\tthis work is to provide a prospective tool to be used as a\n541\tcomplement to a given supervised detection model to help\n542\tidentify new AI services that would evade its radars, not to\n543\treplace it. For instance, this may be used to raise an alarm\n544\ton a swarm of undetected synthetic music all coming from\n545\tthe same record label or artist.\n546\tTask B: multiclass clustering. We now move to the task B\n547\tand the variant on the method that involves clustering. We\n548\tfind that the best way to present the result is to display the\n549\tcomputed clusterings in Figure 2. We indicate the purity\n550\tratio in each cluster (i. e., the ratio of the major label in a\n551\tcluster) as an indication of consistency.\n552\tOn the FMA-AE dataset, the five classes are almost perfectly separated, with some punctual outliers (indicated\n553\twith the less than 100% purity).\n554\tOn the Echoes dataset, almost all classes are well\n555\tseparated, except for the cluster c7 of mixed class\n556\treal+Mubert, as well as another mixed cluster c3 of\n557\tSuno+Brev. The 75.6% purity in the real class (instead of\n558\thalf), is due to the class size imbalance of the dataset (i. e.,\n559\t\n560\t\f3 times more real samples than Mubert ones). The confounding on Mubert is consistent with our previous experiments, which showed that fakeprints from Mubert were not\n561\tlinearly separable from those of the real class. We were curious about the mix of Brev and Suno. After searching online, we found out that \"Brev.ai [...] uses Suno V3.5 technology to create original music from text descriptions.\" 4\n562\tOur aim was to potentially detect sub-versions of models\n563\tto get a better granularity than the ground-truth labels. It\n564\tturns out our method may also be used to identify services\n565\tthat rely on the same generator, which was an unanticipated but interesting side-effect.\n566\tOn the third PopularAISet, almost all models are properly clustered. Interestingly here, Suno samples are split\n567\tinto two clusters c3 and c2. After manual checking, all\n568\tsamples in c3 correspond to Suno-v3.5, while c2 contains\n569\tSuno-v4.5 and Suno-v5 samples. This suggests that the artifact patterns are different between versions, which seems\n570\tto indicate a change in model architecture. As anticipated\n571\tfrom the separability tests, the cluster c6 contains a mix of\n572\treal+Mureka samples. Surprisingly, one subset of Mureka\n573\tsamples are clustered away in c1. We manually inspected\n574\tthe files and found out that all these were generated with\n575\tthe latest version V9 of Mureka (differently from the other\n576\tdataset samples generated with V7.6, V8 and O2). We wonder if the fact that it is the latest version of this service\n577\tthat includes detectable artifacts in the fakeprints could be\n578\tlinked to the use of a watermarking technology by the service to flag their generations, or if this is purely linked to\n579\tarchitectural changes.\n580\tAll in all, our proposed approach shows good properties on these three datasets. As a reminder, all of this was\n581\tdone in a purely zero-shot fashion. The metadata and version information from the PopularAISet were only checked\n582\tafterward to interpret the resulting clustering.\n583\t\n584\t(a) FMA-AE.\n585\t\n586\t(b) Echoes.\n587\t\n588\t5. CONCLUSION\n589\tWe show that previous work conducted on AI music detection can be well extended to the zero-shot territory. Our\n590\tmethod is fast, does not use deep learning, and can serve\n591\tas a complement to existing supervised detectors to track\n592\tnewly released versions and models, paving the way for\n593\tthe exhaustive monitoring of AI-generated music in deliveries to large-scale catalogs. One limitation of our work is\n594\tthe need for a sufficiently large sample collection for a synthetic class to detect it (otherwise, it is treated as noise and\n595\tthus as real). In future work, we may want to investigate\n596\tthe shape in the fakeprints themselves (e. g., detecting periodicities). We should also further investigate why samples\n597\tfrom Mureka (before v9) and Mubert do not seem to be detected by the fakeprints and improve this technique. Other\n598\tfuture work may include addressing robustness to hybrid\n599\tcontent, an open question in this field.\n600\t\n601\t4 brev.ai/ai-song-generator\n602\t\n603\t(c) PopularAISet.\n604\t\n605\tFigure 2: Clustering results for task B. The colors indicate the ground-truth classes for each point. The ellipses\n606\tare drawn around each cluster computed by the HDBSCAN. A cluster index \"cX\" is displayed for reference, as\n607\twell as a purity score for each cluster.\n608\t\n609\t\f6. REFERENCES\n610\t[1] Deezer, “Deezer deploys cutting-edge AI detection\n611\ttool for music streaming,” https://newsroom-deezer.\n612\tcom/2025/04/deezer-reveals-18-of-all-new-music-u\n613\tploaded-to-streaming-is-fully-ai-generated/, 2025,\n614\t[Online; accessed 21-April-2026].\n615\t[2] ——, “Deezer deploys cutting-edge AI detection tool\n616\tfor music streaming,” https://newsroom-deezer.com/\n617\t2026/04/ai-generated-tracks-represent-44-of-new\n618\t-uploaded-music/, 2026, [Online; accessed 21-April2026].\n619\t[3] H. H. Jiang, L. Brown, J. Cheng, M. Khan, A. Gupta,\n620\tD. Workman, A. Hanna, J. Flowers, and T. Gebru, “AI\n621\tArt and its Impact on Artists,” in AIES. ACM, 2023.\n622\t[4] T. S. Goetze, “AI Art is Theft: Labour, Extraction, and\n623\tExploitation: Or, On the Dangers of Stochastic Pollocks,” in ACM FAccT, 2024.\n624\t[5] Y. Wei, Y. Zhu, P. Hui, and G. Tyson, “Exploring the\n625\tUse of Abusive Generative AI Models on Civitai,” in\n626\tProceedings of the 32nd ACM International Conference on Multimedia, 2024, pp. 6949–6958.\n627\t[6] Deezer, “Deezer confirms demonetization of up to 85%\n628\tof AI-music streams due to fraud and moves to sell AIdetection Technology,” https://newsroom-deezer.com/\n629\t2026/01/ai-generated-music-deezer-selling-detection\n630\t-tool/, 2026, [Online; accessed 21-April-2026].\n631\t[7] European Comission, “EU AI Act,” https://artificialint\n632\telligenceact.eu/article/50/, 2024, Article 50.\n633